WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.475
Agents: end state versus trajectory.

2
00:00:02.755 --> 00:00:08.195
An agent takes many steps: it reads, calls tools, changes things, and talks to the user.

3
00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:09.968
You can grade two things.

4
00:00:10.248 --> 00:00:20.147
The end state (outcome) is what the world looks like afterward: is there a reservation in the database, did the tests pass, was the ticket closed with the right code?

5
00:00:20.427 --> 00:00:28.384
The trajectory (transcript or trace) is the path: which tools it called, in what order, with what arguments, and what it said along the way.

6
00:00:28.664 --> 00:00:33.827
Anthropic’s guide to agent evals recommends grading outcomes in most cases.

7
00:00:34.107 --> 00:00:45.712
An outcome check is closer to what users care about, and checking for a rigid sequence of tool calls is brittle, because agents often find valid approaches the designer didn’t anticipate.

8
00:00:45.992 --> 00:00:53.565
Its example: a booking agent’s outcome is whether the reservation actually exists, not whether the agent said it booked one.

9
00:00:53.845 --> 00:01:05.941
tau-bench (Yao and colleagues, 2024) grades this way: it compares the final database to an annotated goal state, and checks that the agent told the user the required information.

10
00:01:06.221 --> 00:01:10.360
But the end state is not always enough, and tau-bench’s authors say so.

11
00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:23.056
A correct final database is necessary but not sufficient: an agent might process a return without the user’s explicit confirmation, which violates policy even though the database looks right.

12
00:01:23.336 --> 00:01:29.565
That is the job of trajectory checks: targeted rules about the path that matter regardless of outcome.

13
00:01:29.845 --> 00:01:32.405
Never call a write tool before confirmation.

14
00:01:32.685 --> 00:01:35.736
Always verify identity before touching an account.

15
00:01:36.016 --> 00:01:38.085
Don’t read another customer’s records.

16
00:01:38.365 --> 00:01:40.392
Stay within a step and cost budget.

17
00:01:40.672 --> 00:01:42.613
So the practical design is both.

18
00:01:42.893 --> 00:01:44.728
Grade the end state for success.

19
00:01:45.008 --> 00:01:48.997
Add a small number of trajectory assertions for policy and safety.

20
00:01:49.277 --> 00:01:59.027
Use the full transcript for diagnosis: when a task fails, read it, because, as the guide puts it, you need to know whether the agent or the eval is at fault.

21
00:01:59.307 --> 00:02:10.123
The guide reports a case where fixing grading bugs on a public benchmark moved a model’s score from 42% to 95%, which is what an unread eval can hide.
